A||||5||DavidHenshaw||Headlamp motor relay||||1||
Z||000000||DavidHenshaw||10-21-2000||03:54 PM||||The relay to my right headlamp motor looks like its shot. What is the purpose of the relay and is it possible or advisable to connect the headlamp motor circuit without using the relay.<P>Thanks <P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-21-2000||06:16 PM||||no. you don't want to hook up w/o the relay. You'll likely burn out the contacts in something else. Could burn up the wires if you didn't do it just right.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-21-2000||08:42 PM||||The point of the relay is so that there only had to be one small wire from the headlight switch in the car to the front of the car. The relay, we power is applied from the switch in the cabin when you push the button, will close a connection in the relay and it will run the power needed to either run the headlight motors and or power the lights themselves. Depending on how you are thinking of eliminating the relay, either your lights will be on and up all the time, even with the car off, or they will not work at all because the power comming out of the headlight switch the close the relay is not enough to power the device itself. Relays are pretty cheap, auto parts stores rip you off for like $12. When I can get the same things thru the company my stereo shop orders from to install car alarm systems for about $3 a peice. Really $12 isnt bad if you only need to replace one. The relay cant be eliminated unless you wish to upgrade to the 87+ headlight system, they are controlled thru an electronic system as opposed to relays.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||DavidHenshaw||10-22-2000||09:03 PM||||Thanks for the info. Purchased original equipment relay from Advance Auto $18(ouch), plugged it in and glory be it works. That doesn't happen very often.<P>One note. It really is a good idea to disconnect the battery before working on anything electrical. As soon as I plugged in the relay the lamp cycled down almost catching my hand. That was close.<P>Dave<P>86 GT fastback||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||10-23-2000||08:39 AM||||I used Oliver Scholz's very good write-up on diagnosing headlight problems to identify a bad isolation relay on my new 85GT.  His procedure is very straight forward and easy to follow.  See it at:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A> <P>The isolation relay that was on the car matches the part number in the 22P manual.  My 85SE and the parts car I got the new headlights from both have the same different part number isolation relay.<P>I'm having a hard time finding a replacement at parts stores (dealer parts dept. not open on weekends).  I guess I'll go to the dealer this week.<P>Seanh, do you know the part number for the relay you can get through your connections?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||10-23-2000||11:23 AM||||I am unsure of the part number or anything. They are the ones used where constant power is applied to one terminal, low power to switch the relay, and the power wire going to the device you want to turn on. Let me look today, they are the ones with like 85,86,87 and some other number on them. Ill look tonight. Would anyone be interested in some if I could buy them?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
